{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And to the sons of the concubines that Abraham had.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: it is written פילגשם (his concubine, singular) — and this is Keturah, whom he mentioned above. And she is called concubine because she is of a servant family, and she is called wife because he wrote her a ketubah. And thus our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'wives — with a ketubah; concubines — without a ketubah.' And so it is written in Chronicles: ״ובני קטורה פילגש אברהם״. And the reason Scripture did not specify whose daughter she was: some explained that since she was a Canaanitess, therefore it was brief in tracing her lineage; for if she had been a Philistine or an Egyptian or from any other nation, Scripture would have specified her lineage, as we find in the case of Esau (Genesis 28:9): ״ויקח את מחלת בת ישמעאל בן אברהם״. And because she was from cursed seed, therefore Scripture was brief and left it unspecified and did not explain." "<b>And to.</b> Let it not be difficult in your eyes to say, how could he take for himself a Canaanitess as a wife, when he is the one who warned and made his servant swear not to take a wife for his son from the daughters of Canaan? The reason is that the concern applied only to Isaac, who was the principal seed, as it is written (Genesis 21:12): ״כי ביצחק יקרא לך זרע״. And since the purpose was fulfilled in Isaac, there is no need to be concerned about Abraham if he marries a Canaanitess." "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> This Keturah is Hagar. And why was her name called Keturah? Because her deeds were beautiful as ketoret (incense). And according to this Midrash it comes out correctly: ״ויוסף אברהם״ — for she was already his maidservant during Sarah's lifetime, and he begot Ishmael from her; and now, when he brought her back, it says of her ויוסף ('and he added'), in the language of ויוסף לדעתה."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:01.223851766Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}